  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nSpotty morning showers and storms drifted across eastern Nebraska \nand western Iowa early this morning, before dissipating and drifting \neast of the area. Cloud cover lingering through the morning kept \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 80s, but as clouds clear, temps are \nexpected to quickly rise through the mid to upper 90s this \nafternoon. Dewpoints reaching the low to mid 70s will allow heat \nindices to rise up to 100-105.\n\nMostly clear skies and well above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough tomorrow. Overnight lows will only dip into the mid and \nupper 70s tonight, before rising back into the mid 90s on Friday \nafternoon.\n\nThe upper level pattern will continue to be dominated by a large \nridge over the nation's mid section this weekend and into next week. \nTemperatures will continue to remain well above seasonal normals, \nwith highs forecast to remain in the 90s through midweek. The \ncumulative effects of back to back hot days, and little relief with \novernight lows in the low to mid 70s, will continue to push Heat \nRisk into the Major to Extreme categories.\n\nA few shortwaves rounding the ridge could bring us off and on \nchances for storms to drift in from the west, dotting the forecast \nwith 20-30% pops primarily in the morning and evening hours. \nUnfortunately, outside of these hit or miss storms, substantial \nwidespread precipitation looks unlikely over the next several days. \nOn the positive side, any cloud cover from these showers and storms \nwould likely moderate temperatures a few degrees. However, the \ncumulative effects of heat indices near 100-105 for multiple days in \na row, combined with temperatures only falling into the mid 70s \novernight necessitate an extension to the Heat Advisory through at \nleast Saturday. Sunday and Monday look a few degrees cooler for the \nmoment, however Tuesday heat indices pop right back into the low \n100s. Given the holiday weekend with several outdoor activities, \nfurther extension may be needed where the hottest temperatures \npersist. \n\nLonger range models continue to hint at some potential relief from \nthe heat by the middle of next week, as a cold front drifts through \nthe region Tuesday. Unfortunately, that means highs only in the mid \nto upper 80s, as opposed to the mid to upper 90s. But, at this \npoint, we'll take whatever relief we can get.",
